 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the paper "Geometry and Attribute Compression for Voxel Scenes" by Timothy Kol et al. from Delft University of Technology. It addresses the limitation of previous DAG-based compression methods that were restricted to single-bit geometry information. The method builds on the concept of DAGs for sparse voxel scenes but extends it to arbitrary data through decoupling and palette compression. It outperforms existing state-of-the-art techniques, such as Moxel DAGs, which require more memory for indices and lack multi-resolution support.
+The method was introduced in the paper "Geometry and Attribute Compression for Voxel Scenes" by Timothy Kol et al. from Delft University of Technology [S1]. It addresses the limitation of previous DAG-based compression methods that were restricted to single-bit geometry information [S1]. The method builds on the concept of DAGs for sparse voxel scenes but extends it to arbitrary data through decoupling and palette compression [S1]. It outperforms existing state-of-the-art techniques, such as Moxel DAGs, which require more memory for indices and lack multi-resolution support [S1]. Moxel DAGs store the number of empty leaf voxels in the first child's subtree and use two running sums to compute indices, leading to large storage requirements for the index per node as empty leaves grow to quadrillions, whereas this method uses only one running sum and decouples attributes [S1].
 
